## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why this breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`).

---

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` or timeout |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (migrated from ThreadAbortException handler)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to loop condition check
                // or break out if you want immediate exit:
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 5 seconds for graceful shutdown
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

        // If thread is still alive, it's stuck in uncooperative code.
        // There is NO way to force-kill it in modern .NET.
        // Options: redesign the blocking call, or move to a child process.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| `GetNextItem()` blocks forever, ignoring cancellation | Pass `CancellationToken` to it — use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne()` instead of `Thread.Sleep`, or async I/O with token |
| Forgetting to pass token through the call chain | Every blocking/long-running method must accept and check the token |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` as a "grace period" | Use `_workerThread.Join(timeout)` — it waits AND detects completion |
| Expecting instant cancellation | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations |
| Leaking `CancellationTokenSource` | Always `Dispose()` it — implement `IDisposable` on the containing class |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions |

---

### If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a queue

Replace manual blocking with `BlockingCollection<T>` which natively supports cancellation:

```csharp
private BlockingCollection<WorkItem> _queue = new();

private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}
```

---

### Verification Checklist

- ✅ No `Thread.Abort` / `ThreadAbortException` / `Thread.ResetAbort` references
- ✅ No `SYSLIB0006` warnings
- ✅ `CancellationToken` passed through full call chain
- ✅ `CancellationTokenSource` disposed
- ✅ Graceful shutdown with bounded `Join` timeout
